<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ntshingwayo Khoza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1809–<a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ntshingwayo kaMahole</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ntshingwayo kaMahole of the Khoza was the commanding general, inDuna, of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-zulu-war">Anglo-Zulu War</a> of 1879. Given conflicting orders, to destroy the invading British centre column but first to seek a parley, he outmanoeuvred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-thesiger-2nd-baron-chelmsford">Lord Chelmsford</a>, whose divided force left its camp poorly defended, and on 22 January 1879 his regiments virtually annihilated it at Isandlwana, where some 1,300 died in one of the worst British defeats of the Victorian era. Blamed for the heavy Zulu losses, he lost much of the acclaim due to him. On 21 July 1883 he was killed when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zibhebhu-kamaphitha">Zibhebhu kaMaphitha</a> attacked Cetshwayo's royal homestead at oNdini.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ntshingwayo-khoza.jpg" alt="Ntshingwayo Khoza" width="330" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer (Life time: Unknown, but late 1800s.) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nthsingwayo_kaMahole.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ndlela kaSompisi, a general of Shaka and Dingane, was Dingane’s chief adviser; <b>Ntshingwayo</b> kaMahole commanded the army that won at Isandlwana.</blockquote><a href="/societies/zulu-kingdom">Social Hierarchy of the Zulu Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
